<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Merged into the generated Info.plist by Tauri, which picks up any
    Info.plist sitting beside tauri.conf.json. That includes the iOS build,
    which merges this file and Info.ios.plist, so keys here reach both
    platforms; Info.ios.plist is only for keys macOS must not get.

    Without NSCameraUsageDescription macOS does not merely refuse the camera —
    it does not expose the API at all, so `navigator.mediaDevices` is undefined
    and the client reports "Camera access is unavailable in this browser"
    rather than a denial. The string is shown to the user in the system prompt,
    so it has to say what the camera is for.

    wry's WKUIDelegate already answers the webview-level permission with
    `Grant`, so this key is the only thing missing on macOS. Under hardened
    runtime — which notarization requires — the signed app will additionally
    need the `com.apple.security.device.camera` entitlement; see README.

    On iOS the camera also backs the composer's attachment picker ("Take Photo
    or Video"), so the string covers both uses.

    NSMicrophoneUsageDescription is for that picker's "Video" mode, which
    records audio. iOS does not refuse the microphone when the key is missing:
    it terminates the app the moment the picker asks, so choosing "Video"
    crashed the iOS build while "Photo" (camera only) worked. On macOS the key
    is inert until something records audio; a signed build doing so would also
    need the `com.apple.security.device.audio-input` entitlement.
  -->
  <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Axon uses the camera to scan a Matrix sign-in QR code and to take photos and videos to send.</string>
  <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Axon uses the microphone to record audio when you capture a video to send.</string>
  <key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
  <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
